      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1886, three San Francisco businessmen—including lawyer Frank Morrison Pixley—incorporated the town, naming it after him.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://tularecountyedc.com/city/pixley/",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Southern Pacific railroad made Pixley a mandatory rest stop, expanding a freight dock into a depot and a three‑story hotel.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://tularecountyedc.com/city/pixley/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The first house in Pixley was built for Emma, William Pixley’s widow, the late brother of Frank Pixley, marking the town’s early domestic life.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pixley%2C_California",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "editorial_filler"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "When the Southern Pacific extended its tracks to Pixley, the town prospered, turning a speculative settlement into a thriving community.",
